Use a fitted calibration without discarding coefficient uncertainty (@met-regression-inverse: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @metrology, #source "../sources/Statistical-Handbook.lingua#r_6S3221KSX1KKGPJNXKC3N022NN", #source "../sources/GUM-Models.lingua#r_3AAJPGTHAAF72R05SJ1VJ7HN46", #source "../sources/Uncertainty-NIST-1900.lingua#r_75WCVW8CJG4TAFD3330T87588E", #metrology "Resolution-and-quantization.lingua#r_1XRZT4EC7VWK34F5JXD8MXRQF2", #industrial-gases-and-vacuum "../../../utilities/industrial-gases-vacuum-and-high-purity-supplies/measurement/Base-subtraction-and-a-defined-calibration-factor.lingua#r_3Z5TE7BDQKX9ZFTNV5903W121P", #clean-manufacturing "../../../production/clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control/aerosol-measurement/A-dilution-test-checks-proportionality-without-fixing-the-scale.lingua#r_06SM1PSEGEFSGMMDASER4Z4PN4") { r_33VGQWJCXFMMX2TJ245VDMHS4N
**Read:** [Measurement Process Characterization — NIST/SEMATECH](../sources/Statistical-Handbook.lingua#r_6S3221KSX1KKGPJNXKC3N022NN), instrument calibration and regression uncertainty; [Developing and using measurement models — JCGM GUM-6](../sources/GUM-Models.lingua#r_3AAJPGTHAAF72R05SJ1VJ7HN46), clauses 8.3 and 11; [Simple Guide for Evaluating and Expressing Uncertainty — Possolo](../sources/Uncertainty-NIST-1900.lingua#r_75WCVW8CJG4TAFD3330T87588E), sections 4–7 and E7 Thermistor Calibration.

Fitted coefficients are estimates with uncertainty and often covariance. Under a simple independent equal-variance linear-response model with negligible x uncertainty, residual variance estimate s² gives u²(a) = s²/Sxx, u²(b) = s²(1/n + x̄²/Sxx), and cov(a,b) = −x̄s²/Sxx, where Sxx = Σ(x_i − x̄)². These expressions belong to that model, not every calibration.

For an unknown input inferred from indication y, x = (y − b)/a requires a nonzero usable sensitivity. Its local sensitivities are 1/a to y, −1/a to b, and −x/a to a. Include coefficient covariance and the relation between a new indication and the calibration data where relevant. Treating a and b as independent can alter the uncertainty substantially.

Centering reference levels around their mean changes the parametrization and can simplify covariance, but does not create new information. Predictions far from the reference range can have larger uncertainty and weak physical support. Repeated readings of the unknown do not make the fitted calibration relation exact.

**Work:** derive the three sensitivities, propagate a stated teaching covariance matrix, and verify the result by joint simulation or direct perturbation. Compare estimates near the calibration centre and near its extremes. Then make the slope small and explain why the inverse problem becomes ill-conditioned. Confirm that the covariance matrix is mathematically valid before drawing correlated samples.

Study an errors-in-variables or observation-model treatment when uncertainty in the reference axis is important. Compare it with the simplified regression rather than declaring one formula universally correct. This distinction is essential when a calibration standard is a major contributor rather than an effectively exact design value.

**Continue:** [Resolution, rounding, thresholds, and when repetition helps](Resolution-and-quantization.lingua#r_1XRZT4EC7VWK34F5JXD8MXRQF2).

